1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,083
This video shows how to make a D F A as small as possible. The method is called D F A minimization.

2
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:23,618
First we find out when two states are the same. Then we learn the algorithm: merge everything, then split when forced. We run it on a or a b star and on f optional g star. At the end we solve two past midterm questions.

3
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:33,476
A lexer is compiled from regular expressions in three steps. Step 1 turns a regex into an N F A, with the templates.

4
00:00:33,926 --> 00:00:39,089
Step 2 turns the N F A into a D F A, with the subset construction.

5
00:00:39,539 --> 00:00:45,533
The subset construction gives a correct D F A. But it often has more states than it needs.

6
00:00:45,983 --> 00:00:47,882
Step 3 is minimization.

7
00:00:48,332 --> 00:00:52,876
It gives a D F A for the same language, with the smallest number of states.

8
00:00:53,326 --> 00:00:59,086
Here is the D F A for a or a b star, from the last video. It has four states.

9
00:00:59,536 --> 00:01:02,544
B and C accept. They have a double border.

10
00:01:02,994 --> 00:01:06,215
D is the empty set. It is the error state.

11
00:01:06,665 --> 00:01:11,807
Two of these four states could be one state. Which two? And how can you show it?

12
00:01:12,257 --> 00:01:15,073
The answer is B and C. Both accept.

13
00:01:15,523 --> 00:01:17,208
On a, both go to D.

14
00:01:17,658 --> 00:01:19,493
On b, both go to C.

15
00:01:19,943 --> 00:01:26,343
So no string that comes after B or C can tell them apart. We now make this idea exact.

16
00:01:26,793 --> 00:01:36,307
When are two states the same? Not when they look alike. Two states p and q are equivalent if nothing that happens after them can tell them apart.

17
00:01:36,757 --> 00:01:45,696
Formally: for every string w, the run from p on w accepts exactly when the run from q on w accepts.

18
00:01:46,146 --> 00:01:53,805
In the picture, p reads w and q reads w. The two runs give the same answer, for every w.

19
00:01:54,255 --> 00:02:02,959
A string w that accepts from one state and not from the other is a witness. A witness shows that the two states must stay apart.

20
00:02:03,409 --> 00:02:13,883
Equivalence is a congruence. If p is equivalent to q, then for every letter x, delta of p and x is equivalent to delta of q and x.

21
00:02:14,333 --> 00:02:22,973
This is why the merged machine is well defined. The arrow of a merged state does not depend on which state of the block we pick.

22
00:02:23,423 --> 00:02:29,695
An exercise from the slides. Here is a D F A with four states, p, q, r and s.

23
00:02:30,145 --> 00:02:31,724
Only r accepts.

24
00:02:32,174 --> 00:02:39,918
Is p equivalent to q? Is p equivalent to s? For each pair, give a witness, or explain why there is none.

25
00:02:40,368 --> 00:02:42,011
Pause the video and try it.

26
00:02:42,461 --> 00:02:47,559
First, p and q. We look for a string that accepts from one and not from the other.

27
00:02:48,009 --> 00:02:53,855
Try the empty string first. With no input, p stays in p, and q stays in q.

28
00:02:54,305 --> 00:02:58,422
Neither p nor q accepts. So epsilon is no witness.

29
00:02:58,872 --> 00:03:03,203
Now try b. From p, b loops back to p, which rejects.

30
00:03:03,653 --> 00:03:06,405
From q, b goes to r, which accepts.

31
00:03:06,855 --> 00:03:13,596
One run accepts and the other rejects. So b is a witness, and p is not equivalent to q.

32
00:03:14,046 --> 00:03:20,446
Now p and s. Here we cannot try every string. We must argue that no witness exists.

33
00:03:20,896 --> 00:03:24,203
On b, p stays in p, and s stays in s.

34
00:03:24,653 --> 00:03:30,434
Neither p nor s accepts. So any run of b's gives the same answer from both.

35
00:03:30,884 --> 00:03:33,252
On a, both p and s go to q.

36
00:03:33,702 --> 00:03:40,913
From that point, the two runs are in the same state, q. So they are the same run, and they give the same answer.

37
00:03:41,363 --> 00:03:48,957
So no witness exists, and p is equivalent to s. Both accept exactly the strings that end in a b.

38
00:03:49,407 --> 00:03:56,639
We cannot find equivalent states by trying all strings. There are too many. So we work the other way round.

39
00:03:57,089 --> 00:04:11,361
Step 1. Assume that all states are equivalent until they are proved different. Two states are already different if one accepts and the other does not. Here only r accepts. The empty string is the witness.

40
00:04:11,811 --> 00:04:15,203
So start with two blocks: r, and p, q, s.

41
00:04:15,653 --> 00:04:23,483
Step 2. Split when a letter forces it. On b, q goes to r, but p and s stay in the block p, q, s.

42
00:04:23,933 --> 00:04:33,085
If a letter takes two states of a block into different blocks, some suffix separates them. So the block must come apart: p s, and q.

43
00:04:33,535 --> 00:04:38,953
Step 3. Stop when no letter forces a split. What is left is equivalence.

44
00:04:39,403 --> 00:04:42,817
Here, p and s stay together, as we found before.

45
00:04:43,267 --> 00:04:56,152
Here is the same method in the form you run with a pen. Step 1. Draw one bubble for all accepting states, and one for all the rest. Here: the bubble r, and the bubble p q s.

46
00:04:56,602 --> 00:05:01,914
If there is an error state, it gets its own bubble. This machine has no error state.

47
00:05:02,364 --> 00:05:07,057
Step 2. Draw the transitions between bubbles, one per state and letter.

48
00:05:07,507 --> 00:05:17,491
The result is usually not a legal D F A. Here the bubble p q s has two arrows labelled b: one back to itself, and one to r.

49
00:05:17,941 --> 00:05:23,147
Two arrows with the same label from one bubble are an ambiguous transition.

50
00:05:23,597 --> 00:05:33,154
Step 3. Pick an ambiguous transition and fix it. Split its source bubble into the states that go one way and the states that go the other way.

51
00:05:33,604 --> 00:05:38,191
On b, q goes to r, but p and s stay in the bubble p q s.

52
00:05:38,641 --> 00:05:41,414
So the bubble splits into p s, and q.

53
00:05:41,864 --> 00:05:48,691
Step 4. Repeat until no ambiguity is left. Now every bubble has one arrow per letter.

54
00:05:49,141 --> 00:05:57,567
It is a D F A again, and it is the smallest one. When you do it by hand, you may choose which ambiguity to fix first.

55
00:05:58,017 --> 00:06:05,121
Here is the same algorithm as pseudo code. It is Moore's algorithm, from 1956.

56
00:06:05,571 --> 00:06:11,054
The machine M must be total and trim. The next slide explains these two words.

57
00:06:11,504 --> 00:06:17,733
Start with two blocks: the accepting states F, and the rest. Drop a block if it is empty.

58
00:06:18,183 --> 00:06:21,682
Then repeat. For each block, look at each state q.

59
00:06:22,132 --> 00:06:29,876
For each letter x, write down the block that delta of q and x is in. This list is the signature of q.

60
00:06:30,326 --> 00:06:34,102
Split the block into groups of states with the same signature.

61
00:06:34,552 --> 00:06:38,563
In the picture, q 1 and q 2 are in the same block B.

62
00:06:39,013 --> 00:06:43,983
But x takes q 1 into block B 1, and q 2 into block B 2.

63
00:06:44,433 --> 00:06:49,041
So q 1 and q 2 have different signatures, and the block splits.

64
00:06:49,491 --> 00:06:51,710
Stop when a round makes no split.

65
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:57,323
Each block becomes one state. The start state is the block of q 0.

66
00:06:57,773 --> 00:07:02,658
The arrow from a block on x goes to the block of delta of q and x.

67
00:07:03,108 --> 00:07:06,073
The accepting states are the blocks inside F.

68
00:07:06,523 --> 00:07:12,539
There are at most as many rounds as states, because each round that splits adds one block or more.

69
00:07:12,989 --> 00:07:17,107
Moore's algorithm needs two conditions on the input machine.

70
00:07:17,557 --> 00:07:22,677
First, the machine must be total. Every state has an arrow on every letter.

71
00:07:23,127 --> 00:07:29,719
The error state must be explicit: a rejecting sink, drawn like any other state. Here it is X.

72
00:07:30,169 --> 00:07:38,446
Without the sink, goes nowhere on f and loops on f look the same to the algorithm. We see this counterexample soon.

73
00:07:38,896 --> 00:07:43,653
Second, the machine must be trim. Every state is reachable from the start.

74
00:07:44,103 --> 00:07:45,895
Here no string reaches U.

75
00:07:46,345 --> 00:07:53,151
No string can tell unreachable states apart. So they merge into any block, and they corrupt the answer.

76
00:07:53,601 --> 00:08:02,667
The subset construction gives you both conditions for free. The empty set is the sink. And every set it builds was reached from the start.

77
00:08:03,117 --> 00:08:10,264
Now minimize our D F A for a or a b star. It is total, because D is the explicit error state.

78
00:08:10,714 --> 00:08:16,325
And it is trim. The start state A reaches B on a, C on a b, and D on b.

79
00:08:16,775 --> 00:08:22,343
Round 0. The accepting states are B and C. They form the bubble B C.

80
00:08:22,793 --> 00:08:27,379
The non accepting states are A and D. They form the bubble A D.

81
00:08:27,829 --> 00:08:31,776
Draw the arrows. On a, A goes to B, in the bubble B C.

82
00:08:32,226 --> 00:08:35,149
But D on a goes to D, in the bubble A D.

83
00:08:35,599 --> 00:08:41,081
So the bubble A D has two arrows labelled a. That is an ambiguous transition.

84
00:08:41,531 --> 00:08:47,782
On b, A and D both go to D, in the bubble A D. So the b arrow of A D is fine.

85
00:08:48,232 --> 00:08:52,904
B and C on a both go to D. So B C on a goes to A D.

86
00:08:53,354 --> 00:09:00,949
On b, B and C go to C. So B C stays in B C on b. These arrows are not ambiguous.

87
00:09:01,399 --> 00:09:08,951
Round 1 in table form. Each state gets one row. The block column holds its block from round 0.

88
00:09:09,401 --> 00:09:12,921
A on a goes to B, in the accepting block B C.

89
00:09:13,371 --> 00:09:16,293
A on b goes to D, in the block A D.

90
00:09:16,743 --> 00:09:19,581
D on a goes to D, in the block A D.

91
00:09:20,031 --> 00:09:23,593
D on b also goes to D, in the block A D.

92
00:09:24,043 --> 00:09:30,785
A and D disagree on a. A goes to an accepting block, and D goes to a non accepting block.

93
00:09:31,235 --> 00:09:36,717
The witness is the string a. So the split is forced: A D becomes A, and D.

94
00:09:37,167 --> 00:09:42,330
B on a goes to D, and C on a goes to D. Both are in the block A D.

95
00:09:42,780 --> 00:09:48,476
B on b goes to C, and C on b goes to C. Both are in the block B C.

96
00:09:48,926 --> 00:09:54,281
Both letters take B and C into the same blocks. So B and C stay together.

97
00:09:54,731 --> 00:10:00,981
Round 2. The block column now holds the blocks from round 1: A, D, and B C.

98
00:10:01,431 --> 00:10:06,487
B and C both go to D on a, and into B C on b. They agree.

99
00:10:06,937 --> 00:10:11,887
A and D are alone in their blocks. A block with one state cannot split.

100
00:10:12,337 --> 00:10:18,182
Nothing forces a split. The new blocks are the old blocks. So the algorithm stops.

101
00:10:18,632 --> 00:10:22,408
Each block becomes one state. The start state is A.

102
00:10:22,858 --> 00:10:26,314
A on a goes to B C, and A on b goes to D.

103
00:10:26,764 --> 00:10:29,900
B C loops on b, and goes to D on a.

104
00:10:30,350 --> 00:10:32,014
D loops on a and b.

105
00:10:32,464 --> 00:10:40,827
We have two states and a sink. A reaches B C on a, and B C loops on b. So B C stands for a b star.

106
00:10:41,277 --> 00:10:52,690
That is what a or a b star was all along. A python check ran the old D F A, the new one and the regex on every string up to length 8. They agree.

107
00:10:53,140 --> 00:11:02,633
Now the counterexample from the course notes. The regex is f optional, g star: zero or one f, then any number of g's.

108
00:11:03,083 --> 00:11:11,425
The subset construction gives this D F A, if we leave out the error state. Its three states A, B and C all accept.

109
00:11:11,875 --> 00:11:21,155
Now run Moore's algorithm on it. Merge the accepting states. All three states accept, so all three go in one block, A B C.

110
00:11:21,605 --> 00:11:25,679
The result is one state, with a loop on f and a loop on g.

111
00:11:26,129 --> 00:11:32,636
This machine accepts f f g f. Every letter loops on A B C, and A B C accepts.

112
00:11:33,086 --> 00:11:40,233
But f optional g star does not match f f g f, because it has two f's. The result is wrong.

113
00:11:40,683 --> 00:11:44,096
What went wrong? B and C have no arrow on f.

114
00:11:44,546 --> 00:11:52,055
Without the sink, goes nowhere on f and loops on f look the same to the algorithm. And they are not the same.

115
00:11:52,505 --> 00:11:56,345
Fix it: add the explicit error state X before you minimize.

116
00:11:56,795 --> 00:12:00,870
X is a rejecting state, and every letter keeps you in X.

117
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:09,043
If the D F A gets an f in state B, it rejects. We model this with an arrow from B to X on f.

118
00:12:09,493 --> 00:12:11,733
C on f also goes to X.

119
00:12:12,183 --> 00:12:16,620
Now every state has an arrow on every letter. The machine is total.

120
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:26,115
This is the same rule as before. The subset construction gives the empty set as this sink. Keep it until minimization is done.

121
00:12:26,565 --> 00:12:32,517
Round 0: the accepting block A B C, and the block X. Now write the signatures.

122
00:12:32,967 --> 00:12:35,954
A on f goes to B, inside A B C.

123
00:12:36,404 --> 00:12:40,500
But B on f goes to X, and C on f goes to X.

124
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:44,342
On g, all three go to C, inside A B C.

125
00:12:44,792 --> 00:12:47,139
X goes to X on both letters.

126
00:12:47,589 --> 00:12:53,626
So A differs from B and C on f. The block is forced apart into A, and B C.

127
00:12:54,076 --> 00:12:57,169
Round 2, with the blocks A, B C, and X.

128
00:12:57,619 --> 00:13:02,035
B on f goes to X, and on g it goes to C, in B C.

129
00:13:02,485 --> 00:13:06,453
C does the same: f to X, and g to C, in B C.

130
00:13:06,903 --> 00:13:13,837
So B and C agree. A and X are alone in their blocks. Nothing splits, and the algorithm stops.

131
00:13:14,287 --> 00:13:17,615
The result has three states: A, B C, and X.

132
00:13:18,065 --> 00:13:24,763
At the end, you can drop the explicit error state X again. The minimal machine is A and B C.

133
00:13:25,213 --> 00:13:31,613
A python check confirms that it matches f optional g star on every string up to length 8.

134
00:13:32,063 --> 00:13:42,837
Now the example from course notes section 3.6. The regex is d star, then dot d or d dot, then d star. Here d is a digit.

135
00:13:43,287 --> 00:13:48,279
This is the D F A that the subset construction gives. It has seven states.

136
00:13:48,729 --> 00:13:54,126
D, E, F and G accept, because they contain N F A state 14.

137
00:13:54,576 --> 00:13:56,645
A, B and C do not accept.

138
00:13:57,095 --> 00:14:02,535
Many arrows are missing. C, D, E, F and G have no arrow on the dot.

139
00:14:02,985 --> 00:14:09,983
Each missing arrow goes to the error state. In the by hand method, the error state gets its own bubble, X.

140
00:14:10,433 --> 00:14:16,939
Run the by hand method. Draw three bubbles. First, A B C for the non accepting states.

141
00:14:17,389 --> 00:14:20,269
Second, D E F G for the accepting states.

142
00:14:20,719 --> 00:14:22,661
Third, X for the error state.

143
00:14:23,111 --> 00:14:25,244
Draw the arrows between the bubbles.

144
00:14:25,694 --> 00:14:33,545
The bubble A B C is ambiguous on d. A goes to B, and B goes to B. Both stay inside A B C.

145
00:14:33,995 --> 00:14:37,195
But C on d goes to E, in D E F G.

146
00:14:37,645 --> 00:14:46,583
A B C is also ambiguous on the dot. A goes to C, and B goes to D. C has no dot arrow, so it goes to X.

147
00:14:47,033 --> 00:14:50,041
We choose to fix d first, as the notes do.

148
00:14:50,491 --> 00:14:52,731
Split A B C into A B, and C.

149
00:14:53,181 --> 00:14:57,213
A and B can stay together on d, because both go to B.

150
00:14:57,663 --> 00:15:04,255
Now A B is ambiguous on the dot. A goes to C, but B goes to D, an accepting state.

151
00:15:04,705 --> 00:15:08,481
Split A B into A and B. No ambiguity is left.

152
00:15:08,931 --> 00:15:14,606
The minimal D F A has A, B, C, and D E F G, plus the error state X.

153
00:15:15,056 --> 00:15:23,781
Seven states became four, the same as notes figure 3.8 c. Moore's rounds give the same blocks. Python checked this.

154
00:15:24,231 --> 00:15:37,266
Now a past midterm question. Minimize the number of states in this D F A, and draw the result. The question also mentions dotted epsilon edges, but this machine has none.

155
00:15:37,716 --> 00:15:41,897
All six states are final states. Each has a double border.

156
00:15:42,347 --> 00:15:43,990
Pause the video and try it.

157
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:47,811
First check the input. All six states are final.

158
00:15:48,261 --> 00:15:54,789
If we start Moore's algorithm now, round 0 has only one block, and nothing can split it.

159
00:15:55,239 --> 00:15:58,161
The result would be one state with loops on a and b.

160
00:15:58,611 --> 00:16:07,401
That machine accepts b b. The given D F A rejects b b: 1 goes to 2 on b, and 2 has no arrow on b.

161
00:16:07,851 --> 00:16:09,643
So that answer is wrong.

162
00:16:10,093 --> 00:16:14,146
The machine is not total. State 2 has no arrow on b.

163
00:16:14,596 --> 00:16:16,900
State 4 has no arrow on a.

164
00:16:17,350 --> 00:16:26,523
So add an explicit error state E. 2 on b goes to E, 4 on a goes to E, and E loops on a and b. E is not final.

165
00:16:26,973 --> 00:16:31,901
Now round 0 has two blocks: the final states 1 to 6, and E.

166
00:16:32,351 --> 00:16:37,791
Round 1. The blocks from round 0 are the final states 1 to 6, and E.

167
00:16:38,241 --> 00:16:44,428
State 1 on a goes to 4, and on b goes to 2. Both targets are in the final block.

168
00:16:44,878 --> 00:16:49,998
States 3, 5 and 6 also go into the final block, on a and on b.

169
00:16:50,448 --> 00:16:55,483
State 2 on a goes to 3, a final state. But 2 on b goes to E.

170
00:16:55,933 --> 00:16:58,130
So 2 has a different signature.

171
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:03,977
State 4 on a goes to E, and on b it goes to 5. That is a third signature.

172
00:17:04,427 --> 00:17:06,475
E goes to E on both letters.

173
00:17:06,925 --> 00:17:12,792
So the new blocks are 1, 3, 5, 6, then 2 alone, then 4 alone, then E.

174
00:17:13,242 --> 00:17:19,173
Round 2, with the blocks from round 1: 1, 3, 5, 6, then 2, 4, and E.

175
00:17:19,623 --> 00:17:25,489
State 1 on a goes to 4, in the block 4. On b it goes to 2, in the block 2.

176
00:17:25,939 --> 00:17:29,545
State 3 does the same: a to 4, and b to 2.

177
00:17:29,995 --> 00:17:36,501
States 5 and 6 go to 6 on both letters. 6 is in the block 1, 3, 5, 6.

178
00:17:36,951 --> 00:17:42,775
So 1 and 3 differ from 5 and 6. The block splits into 1, 3 and 5, 6.

179
00:17:43,225 --> 00:17:47,001
The other blocks have one state each, so they cannot split.

180
00:17:47,451 --> 00:17:51,739
Round 3, with the blocks 1 3, 2, 4, 5 6, and E.

181
00:17:52,189 --> 00:17:57,416
1 and 3 still agree: a goes to the block 4, and b goes to the block 2.

182
00:17:57,866 --> 00:18:02,837
5 and 6 still agree: both letters go to 6, in the block 5 6.

183
00:18:03,287 --> 00:18:07,148
Nothing splits. The algorithm stops with five blocks.

184
00:18:07,598 --> 00:18:12,931
Each block is one state. X is the block 1, 3. It is the start state.

185
00:18:13,381 --> 00:18:16,624
Y is the block 4, and Z is the block 2.

186
00:18:17,074 --> 00:18:21,149
W is the block 5, 6. And E is the error state.

187
00:18:21,599 --> 00:18:25,225
X on a goes to Y, and X on b goes to Z.

188
00:18:25,675 --> 00:18:27,659
Z on a goes back to X.

189
00:18:28,109 --> 00:18:32,184
Y on b goes to W, and W loops on a and b.

190
00:18:32,634 --> 00:18:35,855
Y on a goes to E, and Z on b goes to E.

191
00:18:36,305 --> 00:18:43,196
The posted solution draws the same four states X, Y, Z and W, without the error state.

192
00:18:43,646 --> 00:18:51,177
You can drop the explicit error state at the end. The slides keep it, so every state has one arrow per letter.

193
00:18:51,627 --> 00:19:03,851
Six states became four, plus the error state. A python check ran the given D F A, our result and the posted solution on every string up to length 10. They agree.

194
00:19:04,301 --> 00:19:13,581
One more past midterm question. Minimize the D F A for U or F or T, star. We built this D F A in the last video.

195
00:19:14,031 --> 00:19:17,039
It has four states, and every state accepts.

196
00:19:17,489 --> 00:19:23,675
Every state has an arrow on every letter. So it is total, and no error state is needed.

197
00:19:24,125 --> 00:19:25,768
Pause the video and try it.

198
00:19:26,218 --> 00:19:33,749
Round 0. All four states are accepting, so they form one block. The other block is empty, so we drop it.

199
00:19:34,199 --> 00:19:38,444
Every letter takes every state into this block. Nothing can split it.

200
00:19:38,894 --> 00:19:48,409
So the minimal D F A has one state, A B C D. It accepts, and it loops on U, F and T. This matches the posted solution.

201
00:19:48,859 --> 00:20:04,859
There are other minimization algorithms. Huffman in 1954 and Moore in 1956 used partition refinement. That is what we did. The naive way costs order n squared times the alphabet size.

202
00:20:05,309 --> 00:20:19,751
Hopcroft, in 1971, uses the same refinement. But he always splits with the smaller half of a block. That costs order n log n times the alphabet size. Production tools use it.

203
00:20:20,201 --> 00:20:40,383
Brzozowski, in 1963: reverse the machine, determinize it, reverse it again, and determinize it again. The result is minimal. It uses two subset constructions and no partition. In the worst case it is exponential. Janusz Brzozowski worked here at Waterloo.

204
00:20:40,833 --> 00:20:52,865
Table filling marks the pairs of states that a suffix separates, one length at a time. The pairs that stay unmarked are equivalent. It is the same algorithm, transposed.

205
00:20:53,315 --> 00:21:01,997
To summarize. Two states are equivalent if no string accepts from one and rejects from the other. A string that does is a witness.

206
00:21:02,447 --> 00:21:07,333
Moore's algorithm starts with two blocks: the accepting states and the rest.

207
00:21:07,783 --> 00:21:16,807
Split a block when a letter takes its states into different blocks. By hand, this is an ambiguous transition. Stop when nothing splits.

208
00:21:17,257 --> 00:21:27,603
The input must be total and trim. Keep the explicit error state until the end. Without it, the f optional g star example gives a wrong answer.

209
00:21:28,053 --> 00:21:41,600
Each block is one state of the minimal D F A. Check the result with test strings. Next video: why the minimal D F A is unique, and how to decide if two regexes are equivalent.
